## Phase 18 — Parts and inventory / 2026-09-01 / phase 18: parts

### Built
Migration `20260902000000_phase18_parts`: `unit_stock.starting_qty`,
`unit_stock.confirmed_at`, `stock_movements.unit_stock_id` +
`stock_movements.technician_id`, `stock_confirmations.unit_id` with `job_id`
made nullable, append-only triggers on `stock_movements` and
`stock_confirmations` (reusing `zippy_reject_mutation()` from Phase 1), and a
backfill of `starting_qty` from `quantity` so the reconciliation holds from the
first run.

Config: `STOCK_VERIFIED_MAX_AGE_MINUTES` (120), `STOCK_AGEING_MAX_AGE_MINUTES`
(480).

- `src/domain/stockConfidence.ts` — `stockConfidence()`, `minutesSince()`. Pure,
  no imports, thresholds passed in.
- `src/commit/stockMovements.ts` — `applyStockMovement()`. The only place a van's
  quantity changes.
- `src/commit/partsConsumption.ts` — `declareConsumption()`. Writes a
  `PARTS_CONSUMED` JobEvent.
- `src/commit/stockConfirmations.ts` — `confirmVanStock()`.
- `src/commit/restockOrders.ts` — `raiseRestockIfEmpty()`.
- `src/commit/vanStock.ts` — `readVanStock()`, the one read.
- `src/api/stock.ts` — `GET /technician/stock`, `POST /technician/stock/confirm`,
  `POST /jobs/:id/parts-used`, `GET /units/:id/stock`.
- `src/auth/permissions.ts` — new resources `vanStock` (TECHNICIAN read+update)
  and `fleetStock` (DISPATCHER, FLEET_MANAGER read).
- Seed: `prisma/seed/depots.ts` (3 depots), `prisma/seed/vanCounts.ts` (staggers
  each van across the three confidence bands, with a StockConfirmation row
  behind each stamp). `prisma/seed/fleet.ts` no longer rewrites `quantity` on
  re-seed.
- Frontend: `public/js/panels/vanStock.js`, the van panel on technician.html,
  `van: { stock, counts }` in state.js, and a `kind` on offline-queue items so a
  parts declaration rides the same queue as a ladder tap.
- `tests/stock.test.ts` — 17 tests, including the phase's reconciliation query.

### Decisions

Decision: `stock_movements.id` has no default; the client mints it.
Why: the phase asks for consumption idempotent on movement id. A client-minted
id is the same id on every retry, so a double tap collides on the primary key.
Alternative: a separate `idempotency_key` column with a unique index.
Why not: two identifiers for one thing, and the PK is already unique.
Tradeoff: the API contract now requires callers to generate ids, and a caller
that generates a fresh one per attempt gets no protection at all.

Decision: one declaration id per tap; each line derives
`<declarationId>:<partId>`.
Why: the phone mints one uuid, exactly as the offline queue already does, and a
three-part declaration replays as one act.
Alternative: an id per line from the client.
Why not: three uuids per tap, and a partial replay that no client produces.
Tradeoff: a line REJECTED on the first attempt cannot be reproduced on replay,
because a rejection writes nothing. The client re-reads the van, which is where
the real state is anyway.

Decision: `quantity` is a cache of the ledger, with `starting_qty` as the
anchor: `starting_qty + SUM(delta) = quantity`.
Why: it makes the number checkable rather than trusted, which is the property
the phase is graded on, and a dispatch decision cannot afford to sum a ledger.
Alternative: derive the quantity on every read.
Why not: Phase 19 calls this inside candidate generation, per option.
Tradeoff: two places hold the number and they can drift. The reconciliation
query is the test that they have not.

Decision: consumption does NOT refresh `confirmed_at`.
Why: fitting a belt is not counting the belts. If it refreshed, every busy van
would read VERIFIED without anybody having looked in it, which inverts the whole
point of the phase.
Alternative: treat a declaration as evidence about the remaining count.
Why not: it is evidence about the part that left, not about what is left.
Tradeoff: a van working all day drifts to UNVERIFIED, which from Phase 19 makes
it a worse candidate. That is the intended pressure — it is what makes counting
the van worth a technician's minute.

Decision: a count that disagrees writes an ADJUSTED movement, never an
overwrite.
Why: the ledger has to keep explaining the number; "somebody found one fewer on
Tuesday" is the row an audit wants.
Alternative: set `quantity` and log separately.
Why not: two writes that can disagree, and the reconciliation would break.
Tradeoff: none worth naming.

Decision: no TRANSFER reason, and no cross-van part movement.
Why: the phase names it as a cliff. It needs a second unit's version, a custody
state while the part is in neither van, a rendezvous the solver would have to
price, and a story for a re-dispatch mid-handover.
Alternative: model it.
Why not: it is a second dispatch problem inside a phase about confidence.
Tradeoff: a real thing technicians do is not representable. Phase 19 prices a
workshop run instead — one leg, one owner.

Decision: two permission resources, `vanStock` and `fleetStock`.
Why: "may I see my own van" and "may I see any van" are different questions.
Alternative: one `stock` resource plus an ownership check in the route.
Why not: it would put an RBAC decision back inside a handler, which is the thing
permissions.ts exists to prevent.
Tradeoff: one more row in the table.

### Concurrency

Scenario: a technician declares a part used while a replenishment lands on the
same shelf.
What races: two writers on `unit_stock.quantity`.
Why dangerous: a lost update makes the cached quantity disagree with the ledger,
and from Phase 19 the cached quantity is what decides whether a van can be sent.
How handled: conditional UPDATE on the version read (optimistic lock, the same
shape as holds.ts), then re-read and retry, up to three attempts.
Assumption relied on: READ COMMITTED, so the re-read after a lost race sees the
winner's committed value rather than the snapshot the transaction started from.

Scenario: a double tap, or an offline queue replaying a declaration.
What races: nothing, and that is the point — the second arrival is a duplicate,
not a competitor.
Why dangerous: a second decrement for one fitted part.
How handled: the movement insert is LAST in the transaction, so a duplicate id
raises P2002 and takes the decrement down with it. The caller answers REPLAYED.
Assumption relied on: the client reuses the id across retries. The offline queue
already mints its key at the tap and reuses it, which is why the parts
declaration was put on that queue rather than given a path of its own.

Scenario: a shelf is emptied twice in one shift.
What races: two restock requests for one empty shelf.
Why dangerous: only mildly — a duplicate line on an operator's list.
How handled: a read for an open (REQUESTED|ORDERED) order inside the same
transaction. Not a unique index, because "open" is a status and a partial unique
index is not expressible in the Prisma schema.
Assumption relied on: both writers are one technician's phone, behind one van.

### Weaknesses introduced

- `findOrCreateShelf` can race: two concurrent movements for a part the van does
  not yet carry both try to create the `unit_stock` row, and the loser's unique
  violation surfaces to the caller as REPLAYED rather than as a retry. Does not
  matter at current scale — one technician per van, and replenishment is not
  concurrent with anything.
- A RestockOrder is written and nothing reads it. There is no route and no panel
  listing open requests; an operator sees an empty van through
  `GET /units/:id/stock` (quantity 0) rather than through the request row. The
  phase forbids building procurement, so this is deliberate, but it is a table
  with a writer and no reader until Phase 19.
- Depots are seeded with coordinates and nothing routes to them yet. They exist
  because Phase 19 needs a coordinate to measure a detour to.
- Parts are not in feasibility. `PART_UNAVAILABLE` is still the Phase 3 stub and
  never fires — by instruction; that is Phase 19.
- The seed's van counts are stamped relative to run time and their ids carry the
  date, so re-running the seed on a later day writes a fresh confirmation. That
  is right for a demo whose subject is age, and wrong for anyone who expected a
  seed to be a fixed point.
- `stock_confirmations.technician_id` is a bare column with no FK, following the
  shape the Phase 1 schema already had. The seed writes the literal `'seed'`
  into it, which no `technicians` row will ever match.

### Concepts now genuinely in use

- Client-minted primary keys as idempotency keys, on a second table and a second
  surface (`technician_actions` was the first).
- A cached aggregate with a reconciliation invariant against an append-only
  ledger — the same shape as the double-entry balance check, applied to counts
  rather than to money.
- Confidence as a function of age, computed on read rather than stored, because
  it changes with the clock and with nothing else.
- Optimistic locking on a third table (units, jobs, now unit_stock).

### Interview angle

"A technician taps 'fitted one' in a basement car park and the phone replays the
queue ten minutes later — what stops that decrementing twice?" The movement id is
minted at the tap and is the primary key, and the insert is last in the
transaction, so the duplicate rolls the decrement back with it.

"Your database says there are two batteries in that van. How much do you trust
it?" Not on its own: the number is a claim whose age is the interesting part, so
it carries the time a human last counted it, and from Phase 19 a count older than
eight hours is treated as no stock at all rather than sending a van that turns
out to be empty.

### What broke

The FK on `stock_movements.unit_stock_id` was first written ON DELETE SET NULL,
which is incompatible with the append-only trigger added in the same migration:
SET NULL issues an UPDATE, and the trigger refuses UPDATEs. Caught while working
out how the test would reset its fixture, before it ran. It is RESTRICT now,
which is also the better answer — history pins the row it explains — and it is
why the test tops its van back up with a REPLENISHED movement instead of
deleting and recreating the shelf.

The seed was rewriting `quantity` on every run (`update: { quantity }`). Harmless
while nothing read inventory; from this phase it would have silently rewritten a
ledger-backed number and broken the reconciliation for every van that had done a
job. It sets stock on create only now.

A smoke script run against the dev database appeared to hang with no output. It
had not hung — the output was buffered and lost when the harness killed it, and
the script had in fact run to completion, which is why `unit-01` afterwards read
as counted four minutes ago instead of the seeded five hours. Re-seeding put the
staggering back. The lesson is the one already in CLAUDE.md about rejected Bash
calls: check the disk before assuming a command did not land.

## Phase 19 — Part-aware feasibility / 2026-09-02 / phase 19: part-aware feasibility

### Built
`src/engine/partSourcing.ts` — the new file the phase turns on. Types
`PartStockSnapshot`, `DepotSnapshot`, `PartsSnapshot`, `PartSourcing`,
`PartSourcingStatus` (`NOT_MODELLED | IN_VAN | WORKSHOP_RUN | UNAVAILABLE`);
functions `believableSkus()`, `faultIsSourced()`, `sourcePartsForPairs()`,
`measureDepotRuns()`, private `missingSkusFor()` / `decide()`; constants
`MAX_PART_SOURCING_DETOUR_MINUTES = 45` and `NO_PARTS_MODELLED`.

`src/engine/candidates.ts` — `DispatchSnapshot.parts`; `DispatchOption` gains
`skillResolvableFaults` and `partSourcing`, and `resolvableFaults` now means
skill AND part. `generateCandidates()` calls `sourcePartsForPairs()` once per
job.

`src/engine/feasibility.ts` — `checkPartAvailability()` is real:
`PART_UNAVAILABLE` when sourcing came back `UNAVAILABLE`. New `arrivalMinutes()`
adds the detour, used by the shift check and the SLA check.

`src/engine/cost.ts` — sixth term `partSourcingCost` (paise) via
`partSourcingCostPaise()`, weighted 0.00002 / 0.0002 / 0.00005 across
FASTEST_RESOLUTION / LOWEST_COST / BALANCED. The detour also enters
`expectedTimeToResolutionMinutes()`.

`src/commit/dispatchSnapshot.ts` — reads `unit_stock` (confidence computed at
the snapshot instant), the standard price list's `part_prices`, and `depots`;
takes an optional `now`.

Migration `20260902010000_phase19_part_sourcing`: `dispatch_options.part_sourcing
JSONB`. Written by `persistSolverRun.ts` for both feasible and rejected options,
selected by `GET /console/jobs/:id/decision`, rendered by
`public/js/panels/inspector.js` (`partSourcingLine()`).

`src/domain/driverNarrative.ts` — `NarrativeContext.sourcingPart`,
`STILL_ON_THE_WAY`, `partSourcingSentence()`. `src/api/driver.ts` reads the
assigned unit's stored sourcing through `isWorkshopRun()`.

Evaluation: `prisma/seed/fleet.ts` exports `unitStockPlanEntries()`;
`evaluation/scenarios/fleet.ts` gains `partsSnapshot()`, `bandedConfidence()`,
`isDepleted()` and a `stockDepletion` knob; a sixth scenario `parts-stockout`;
`evaluation/policies/greedy.ts` `stockBlind()`; `POLICY_ALIASES` for `nearest`
and `global`; `JobRecord.stockoutFailures`, `Metrics.stockoutInducedFailures`
and a `stockout fails` column; ground-truth van stock in `runner.ts` that
decrements as repairs consume it.

Tests: `tests/partSourcing.test.ts` (12), an `option count` block in
`tests/candidates.test.ts`, a real `PART_UNAVAILABLE` block in
`tests/feasibility.test.ts`, `partSourcingCost` in `tests/cost.test.ts`, and two
harness-honesty tests in `tests/evaluation.test.ts`.

### Decisions

**Decision** Resolve part sourcing inside `generateCandidates`, collapsed to one
`PartSourcing` per pair, and assert the option count still equals the pair count.
**Why** The phase's own question. A part source is a genuine choice, and the
naive way to model a choice is a candidate per branch — which multiplies the
assignment matrix by the number of ways to get a part.
**Alternative** A candidate per (unit, technician, source).
**Why not** `jobs x units x technicians x sources` does not finish before the
next call arrives, and the extra columns carry no information the collapse
throws away: within a fixed pair and a fixed job, one source dominates.
**Tradeoff** The collapse is made before the global assignment sees it, so the
solver cannot trade a slightly worse source for one job against a better one for
another. That trade does not exist here anyway — depots have no inventory.

**Decision** UNVERIFIED stock counts as absent. AGEING counts as present.
**Why** The asymmetry of the two mistakes. Believing a part is there when it is
not produces a crew who drives out, opens an empty shelf and leaves; the driver
then waits for a whole second dispatch. Believing it is missing when it is there
costs a detour.
**Alternative** Discount stock by confidence and let the cost function weigh it.
**Why not** That is invariant 3's forbidden shape — a constraint disguised as a
number, which some combination of other terms eventually outvotes.
**Tradeoff** A van whose count is nine hours old is treated as empty even when it
is full. A third of the seeded fleet is in that band by construction.

**Decision** `resolvableFaults` now means skill AND part; the skill-only answer
moved to `skillResolvableFaults`.
**Why** `canResolve(option, fault) = hasSkill ∧ hasPart` is the phase's
definition, and every downstream consumer — the cost function's escalation term,
the harness's first-visit test — was asking "will this crew finish the job",
which was never a question about skill alone.
**Alternative** Leave `resolvableFaults` alone and add a parallel parts flag.
**Why not** Two fields answering one question is how a cost function and a
simulator start disagreeing about what a dispatch achieves.
**Tradeoff** A silent semantic change to a field five files read. The rename of
the old meaning is what makes it reviewable.

**Decision** A sourcing detour longer than `MAX_PART_SOURCING_DETOUR_MINUTES`
(45) is a rejection, not an expensive option.
**Why** Past that line a depot run is a second dispatch wearing a costume, and a
second dispatch is already priced by the escalation term — run by a crew who
does not have to drive the wrong way first.
**Alternative** No ceiling; let `partSourcingCost` sort it out.
**Why not** The weight needed to make a two-hour detour lose reliably is a weight
that acts as a filter, which is the thing invariant 3 forbids.
**Tradeoff** A hand-set constant with no measurement behind it, like the
durations. Measured consequence: 23 `PART_UNAVAILABLE` rejections across a whole
`parts-stockout` run, against 45,618 `CAPABILITY_MISSING`.

**Decision** One depot is priced per job — the one nearest the JOB — costing two
extra road-matrix calls, and only when some pair is actually short of a part.
**Why** Pricing every (van, depot) pair is one call per depot per job. The last
leg is the one the driver waits through, so the depot closest to the vehicle is
the right one to prefer.
**Alternative** Nearest depot per van, or all depots for all vans.
**Why not** Six zones and three depots make the difference small; the round trips
are not.
**Tradeoff** A van already parked beside a depot the job is far from does not get
credit for it.

**Decision** `snapshot.parts === null` means "this picture does not model parts"
and never rejects anything — it is not "the vans are empty".
**Why** Two real callers need it: everything written before this phase, and the
harness's greedy baselines, which are stock-blind on purpose.
**Alternative** Make `parts` required and fail closed on an empty array.
**Why not** Fail-closed answers "we looked and found nothing". "Nobody looked" is
a different fact and conflating them turns every pre-parts caller into a system
that refuses to dispatch.
**Tradeoff** A future caller could forget to fill `parts` in and silently get
part-blind dispatch. Only `buildDispatchSnapshot` builds a production snapshot,
and it always fills it in.

**Decision** The three greedy baselines are blind to stock; the runner is not.
**Why** Holding hard constraints identical across policies is what stops the
comparison being rigged against the baselines. Handing them an inventory model
an incumbent system does not have would rig it the other way.
**Alternative** Give every policy the parts snapshot.
**Why not** `stockoutInducedFailures` would then measure nothing — every policy
would avoid the same stockouts and the parts layer would look free.
**Tradeoff** The baselines are now weaker than they were at Phase 10 on a metric
that did not exist then. Stated in `evaluation/policies/greedy.ts` and pinned by
a test that would fail if somebody "tidied up" the blanking.

**Decision** Part VALUE is in the cost term, not only the detour.
**Why** The same part is consumed whichever crew fits it, but not off the same
shelf: stock already on a van is committed, and a depot run draws a fresh unit
out of inventory some other job may want. Pricing that stops the solver treating
the depot as infinite.
**Alternative** Detour time only.
**Why not** Then a van two minutes from a depot is as good as a stocked van, and
the fleet drains its depots.
**Tradeoff** Double counting against the estimate, which also bills the part.
Nothing reads both numbers, but they are two prices for one object.

### Concurrency
**Scenario** Two dispatchers solve at the same instant and both see the same van
holding the last battery.
**What races** Nothing new. The stock lines are read into the snapshot and the
solver only reads them (invariant 2), so both plans may propose the same van.
**Why dangerous** Both crews would be told to fit a battery that exists once.
**How handled** Not by this phase, and that is worth being explicit about: the
saga's version-checked hold (`src/commit/holds.ts`) means only one of the two
acquires the unit, and one active job per unit (invariant 7) means the loser
never reaches the shelf. Stock is not separately reserved.
**Assumption relied on** One active job per unit. The moment a unit can hold two
jobs, "one battery, two jobs" becomes a real race and `unit_stock.version` — the
column Phase 18 added and this phase still does not use — is what would answer
it.

**Scenario** A technician declares a consumption while a solve is in flight.
**What races** The decrement lands after the snapshot was read, so the plan was
made against a shelf with one more battery on it than exists.
**Why dangerous** A crew is dispatched expecting a part that has just been used.
**How handled** The same way every other staleness in the snapshot is handled —
it is a snapshot, not a live view, and the commit-time conditional update is
what catches a world that moved. The cost of being wrong here is one wasted
visit, which is exactly what `stockoutInducedFailures` measures.
**Assumption relied on** Consumption is rare relative to the solve interval, and
a wasted visit is recoverable.

### Weaknesses introduced
- **A depot has infinite stock.** `measureDepotRuns()` never asks whether the
  depot has the part. Phase 18 made depots deliberately dumb and this phase
  builds on that. At current scale it does not matter — three depots, one city —
  but "the workshop run always succeeds" is the single most optimistic
  assumption in the parts layer.
- **`unit_stock.version` is still unread.** Phase 18 added it for optimistic
  locking that nothing does. Correct today only because invariant 7 makes the
  race impossible.
- **The 45-minute detour ceiling is hand-set**, like the durations, with no data
  behind it. It fires 23 times in a whole scenario, so it is nearly inert — which
  is either the right calibration or an untested one.
- **Part value is priced off the STANDARD list, not the job's pinned list.**
  Deliberate — this is a dispatch input, not a bill — but it means a tenant with
  its own price list is dispatched against somebody else's prices.
- **A snapshot without `parts` silently dispatches part-blind.** Fail-open, in a
  layer whose whole argument is fail-closed. Justified for the two callers that
  need it and dangerous if a third appears.
- **Every fixture that creates a unit now needs stock, or a depot within 45
  minutes.** The re-dispatch tests broke on exactly this. It is a real coupling
  between test setup and a filter, and it will bite again.
- **`stockoutInducedFailures` is not a ranking.** It counts crews who had the
  skill and not the part, so a policy that sends MORE qualified crews scores
  worse on it while doing better work. Measured below.

### Concepts now genuinely in use
Fail-closed defaults under uncertainty, and the argument for why the two errors
are not symmetric. Dimensionality collapse inside candidate generation — the
answer to "doesn't that blow up the matrix?". A filter with a threshold
(`MAX_PART_SOURCING_DETOUR_MINUTES`) standing in for a penalty large enough to be
a constraint. Belief versus ground truth in a simulator: the policy sees
confidence, the runner opens the shelf.

### Interview angle
"You added parts to a dispatch problem — why didn't the assignment matrix
explode?" Because the choice of part source is resolved per (unit, technician)
pair inside `generateCandidates` and collapsed to the single best one, so parts
made every option richer and the matrix exactly the same size —
`tests/candidates.test.ts`'s `option count` block asserts it.

"Your evaluation predicted skill-aware would beat nearest on stockouts and it
didn't. What happened?" A stockout-induced failure is defined as a crew who had
the SKILL and not the part, so maximising skill coverage moves failures into that
column that a nearest-unit policy books as capability failures instead — the
metric separates two kinds of wasted visit rather than ranking parts-awareness,
and `EVALUATION.md` finding 4 says so with the numbers.

### What broke
**Six test files went red the moment `PART_UNAVAILABLE` became real** — the
re-dispatch suites most visibly, with a crew that had been feasible for two
phases suddenly producing `WAIT` instead of `RELEASE_PENDING`. The cause was not
the filter: `zippy_test` had been seeded before Phase 18 added depots, so it held
27 parts, 198 stock rows and zero depots. A fixture van with no stock rows of its
own and no depot to divert to is correctly `PART_UNAVAILABLE`. Re-seeding the
test database fixed it. The lesson is the coupling, not the seed: a hard filter
over data a fixture never had to think about turns "my fixture is incomplete"
into "my unrelated test fails".

**The build plan's predicted result did not appear.** It calls for
`stockoutInducedFailures`: nearest > skill-aware > global. The second half holds
everywhere and holds hard (global at 0-27 against baselines at 14-149); the first
half does not — `skill-aware` is WORSE than `nearest-unit` at normal-day (105 v
97), monsoon-surge (149 v 136) and technician-dropout (81 v 74), ties at two, and
wins only at capability-scarcity (122 v 124). Reported in `EVALUATION.md` finding
4 with the reason rather than quietly reweighted, per the phase's own "if it does
not appear, report that too".

**`capability-aware-global` strands 30 of 60 at `parts-stockout` against 2 for
the baselines**, which looked at first like the parts filter refusing everything.
Instrumenting a full run said otherwise: 23 `PART_UNAVAILABLE` against 45,618
`CAPABILITY_MISSING`, 10,120 `UNIT_UNAVAILABLE` and 277 `SLA_UNREACHABLE`. It is
finding 1 again — a job whose window closes can never be dispatched — reached
this time because the stock-aware policy declines to send a crew who cannot
finish while the baselines send them repeatedly (58 dispatched, 22 resolved,
against 30 dispatched and 27 resolved).

**Two long heredocs sent through the Bash tool failed with `unexpected EOF`** at
around 180 lines, twice, writing nothing. Not a shell syntax problem — the same
content written through the Write tool was fine. Anything long goes to a file
first now.
